  "schema:name": "KGPersona",
  "dcterms:title": "Knowledge graph L2: Generic Persona",
  "dcterms:description": "DAK component 2. Grounded in GenericPersona.fsh -- title, id, description, otherNames, iscoCode bound to ISCO08ValueSet -- and in the 22 committed ActorDefinition instances that carry name, title, type and description. The two disagree about where ISCO lives, which the properties record rather than smooth over. `title` is the human-readable label a BPMN pool matches on; `name` is the CamelCase identifier a Canonical() reference matches on. They are different strings for the same persona and conflating them is precisely how the pool join breaks. GenericPersona.fsh declares `iscoCode 0..* code`, but the committed ActorDefinitions record ISCO in prose inside description, so iscoCode is usually empty and is not authoritative. Class IRI: http://smart.who.int/kg/persona.",
